      "body": "## Overview\n\nRun `_insert_resampled_kernel` with `num_warps=1` to avoid a race when reading and updating `num_sampled` in place. The race can cause an out-of-range lookup during speculative decoding and a fatal CUDA exception.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Removes the cross-warp read/write race in `_insert_resampled_kernel` by restricting its execution to one warp.\n- Changes only this kernel's launch configuration.\n- Makes no performance or model-quality claims.\n\n## Validation\n\nTwo CUDA core dumps from separate crashes on an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell show the same failure mechanism:\n\n| Incident | Threads reading `num_sampled = 4` | Threads reading `num_sampled = 5` |\n|---|---|---|\n| First | 0, 32 | 64, 96 |\n| Second | 0, 32, 64 | 96 |\n\nThe listed threads represent different warps within the same block. Disassembly maps these values to the load of `num_sampled`; the corresponding resampling indices are 4 and 5, with an exclusive end index of 5.\n\nBoth dumps report `Warp Out of range Address` at `_insert_resampled_kernel+384`, corresponding to:\n\n    temp = tl.load(temp_ptr + req_state_idx).to(tl.float32)\n\nThe failing environment used vLLM 0.32.0, PyTorch 2.13.0+cu130, Triton 3.7.1, and Gemma 4 MTP with four speculative tokens. The same read/update pattern remains in the main-branch code patched here.\n\nLocal validation:\n- Applicable pre-commit hooks passed, including Ruff, mypy, typos, and repository Python checks.\n- Python syntax validation and `git diff --check` passed.\n\nThe dumps establish the original failure mechanism.\n\n## Details\n\nThe kernel loads `num_sampled`, uses it to compute a resampling index, and increments the same counter in global memory. In the captured machine code, there is no inter-warp barrier between the load and the store.\n\nA delayed warp can therefore read the incremented value after another warp has performed the update. In both incidents, this changes the resampling index from 4 to 5, outside the request's logits range. The subsequent mapping lookup supplies an invalid request-state index, causing the temperature load to fault.\n\nUsing one warp removes this inter-warp dependency without changing the sampling algorithm or introducing another kernel launch.\n\nThis failure occurs before the NaN/argmax handling addressed by #50183.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Pull Request Checklist</summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** GPU correctness, serving validation, and performance checks have passed on real environment.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps the change and its explanatory comment small; applicable static checks passed.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Human review, the duplicate-work check, and the remaining contribution checklist are pending.\n</details>",
